# configs/isp-routes-custom.txt.example
#
# PURPOSE: User-defined CIDRs that bypass VPN and exit via ISP (KEENETIC_GW),
# loaded alongside the auto-downloaded RU list. Use this for foreign services
# that you want to access unmetered or that break under VPN.
#
# PRIORITY: These routes are applied in addition to the auto-downloaded RU list.
# If a CIDR here also appears in the RU list, a duplicate ISP route is silently
# dropped (harmless). If a CIDR here conflicts with vpn-routes-custom.txt,
# vpn-routes-custom.txt wins (applied last).
#
# FORMAT: One CIDR per line. Lines starting with # are comments. No inline
# comments after a CIDR — ip route reads the whole line as the route argument.
#
# HOW TO USE:
#   1. Run: ssh pi4 "sudo /etc/splitgate/vpn-status.sh --via=vpn" to identify
#      IPs exiting via VPN that should go direct
#   2. Resolve to a CIDR (via whois or ipinfo.io)
#   3. cp src/configs/isp-routes-custom.txt.example src/configs/isp-routes-custom.txt
#   4. Add CIDRs (one per line, no inline comments)
#   5. Run: ./src/deploy.sh — Stage 21 deploys to /etc/splitgate/isp-routes-custom.txt
#      and routing.sh Stage 5b loads the routes on activation.
#
# VALIDATION: Malformed lines silently dropped by ip route add (2>/dev/null || true).
#
# EXAMPLE ENTRIES (remove leading # to activate):
# 23.55.0.0/16
# 95.181.176.0/22
